I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern sampling, and the frozen reference/index tooling. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible multiphase GSAS-II model, refine it in checkpoints, and validate that every coherent reflection family is accounted for before exporting the requested files.

The specimen is a Mn–W–O reaction fired at 800 °C (WO₃ + Mn₃O₄, 1:1 metal ratio implied by the lab). I’m now extracting the peak families and querying the frozen, case-specific structure index; I’ll treat MnWO₄ as a hypothesis, not an assumption, and test residual precursor/product phases against the full pattern.

The peak positions give broad family support for monoclinic MnWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃: the strong unshared WO₃ lines at about 23.10, 26.56, 28.60, 33.23, 34.12, and 35.09° are all present, while the MnWO₄ family tracks throughout 15–99°. I’m moving these two phases into an initial whole-pattern refinement; only after that fit will I test whether the weaker residuals support a manganese-oxide precursor phase.

The two-phase refinement is chemically sensible but incomplete (Rwp ≈ 19.7%). Its positive residual contains a broad, internally consistent family near 19.86/20.39, 28.66, 31.78, 35.09, 46.48, 54.44, and 56.46°. I’m testing the complete Mn-oxide and W-oxide reference sets against that residual, including broadened fits, before considering any cross-chemistry analogue.

The broadened reference screen is still running across all oxide entries. The current model gives approximately 82 wt% MnWO₄ and 18 wt% WO₃, but I’m withholding those as final numbers because the unmodeled family is strong enough to bias both scales.

