The analogue screen now covers every three-element W–O structure in the frozen index (not just familiar scheelites). I’m scoring family-wide agreement with penalties for strong predicted lines that are absent; this is the key distinction between a genuine prototype and a one- or two-peak coincidence.

The exhaustive match is compute-heavy but progressing; meanwhile, the simple scheelite assignment already fails a consistency check because its low-angle and 112/004/200 positions cannot be fit by one tetragonal cell. I won’t force that topology solely because several higher-angle peaks coincide.

The pure-Mn-oxide branches are not chemically self-consistent at their fitted scales: the h‑MnO branch alone implies an overall Mn:W ratio around 2.5 instead of the 1:1 charge balance from the dosed precursors. That tells me the broad family is much more likely a W-bearing, Mn-rich oxide. I’m now testing charge-balanced Mn₂WO₆ and Mn₃WO₆ prototypes, where the observed residual WO₃ provides the complementary W-rich fraction.

The residual has now indexed cleanly: 19.857 and 20.390° behave as orthogonal 100/010 lines, 28.605° equals their reciprocal-space sum (110), and 31.778° adds a third axis (111). The same primitive orthorhombic cell, about 4.468 × 4.352 × 6.535 Å, predicts the 45.68, 46.48, 59.32/60.02, 66.42, 74.58, 85.14, and 90.90° family. I’m using that indexed cell—not isolated intensity gains—to locate the correct structural analogue.

The dominant MnWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ phases are established. A coherent residual lattice now points to Mn₂(WO₄)₃ rather than a manganese oxide; I’m testing that full structure against the whole pattern before committing it to the phase inventory.

The residual family is internally consistent with a primitive pseudocubic repeat near 4.4 Å (its 100, 110, 111, 200… sequence is visible across the scan). The Mn₂(WO₄)₃ analogue has the right metric subset but the wrong intensity distribution and only a marginal fit gain, so I’m keeping it as a rejected branch and screening the exact Mn–O and W–O references against that full family.

The chemistry resolves an important ambiguity: a Mn₃WO₆ secondary phase paired with WO₃ restores the nominal 1:1 Mn:W balance, and the Cu₃WO₆-type analogue has the right low-angle and 33–35° reflection clusters. I’m now comparing all physically relevant A₃BO₆ framework analogues, because the published Cu analogue’s coordinates reproduce positions better than intensities and an unbounded size fit became nonphysical.

The rhombohedral Cd₃TeO₆-type analogue is the decisive match. After Cd→Mn and Te→W substitution, it reproduces every residual family from 19.8° through 99°, lowers Rwp from 19.69% to 10.72%, refines to a physical 0.050 µm domain size, and gives provisional fractions 65.5% MnWO₄ / 14.2% WO₃ / 20.3% Mn₃WO₆. I have stopped phase searching and am now refining this accepted three-phase inventory.

